---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Penguin-Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';

const currentDate = new Date();
let currentYear = currentDate.getFullYear();
---

<Layout title="Penguins">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
        <title>Galapagos Penguin</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <header class="header">
            <h1>Galapagos Penguin</h1>
        </header>
        <Navbar/>
        <main>
            <div class="container">
                <nav class="contents">
                    <h2>Contents:</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#diet">Diet</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#behavior">Social Behavior</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#facts">Facts</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </nav>

                <section id="overview">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Scientific Name: Spheniscus mendiculus</li>
                        <li>Size: Approximately 48-53 cm (19-21 inches) tall, weighing 2-2.5 kg (4.4-5.5 lbs).</li>
                        <li>Habitat: Endemic to the Galapagos Islands, Ecuador. They live and breed along the coasts of several islands, often near areas of cool, nutrient-rich currents.</li>
                        <li>Conservation Status: Endangered</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                <section id="diet">
                    <h2>Diet</h2>
                    <p>The Galapagos Penguin feeds primarily on small schooling fish, such as anchovies and sardines, and also some crustaceans. They forage in the cool waters around the islands.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="behavior">
                    <h2>Social Behavior</h2>
                    <p>Galapagos Penguins form small colonies. They are unique among penguins as they can breed year-round, taking advantage of food availability and cooler water temperatures. They exhibit typical penguin social behaviors like vocalizations for communication and pair bonding. They often seek shade to avoid overheating in their equatorial environment.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="facts">
                    <h2>Interesting Facts</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>They are the only penguin species found in the tropics.</li>
                        <li>To cope with the warm climate, they have several adaptations, including panting and holding their flippers out to lose heat.</li>
                        <li>Their breeding is often linked to the cold water upwellings that provide abundant food.</li>
                        <li>They face threats from El Niño events, which can reduce food availability and increase water temperatures.</li>
                        <li>The Galapagos Penguin has a small population and is highly vulnerable to environmental changes and introduced predators.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </div>
            <footer>
                <p><span class="copyleft">&copy;</span> PengiCoder & MrPiggy105, { currentYear }</p>
            </footer>
        </main>
    </body>
</Layout>

<style>
    .contents ul {
        list-style-type: none;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .contents li {
        margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    section {
        overflow: hidden;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        margin-left: 30%;
    }

    h1{
        text-align: center;
        height: 100%;
        font-family: 'Orkney', Arial;
    }
</style>